<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Play 3D Audio and Music in Godot" description="Set up positional 3D audio, ambience, background music, distance falloff, and event-driven playback in Godot 4 and Folded Paper Engine." summary="Good game audio answers three separate questions: where is the sound, when should it play, and who owns its lifetime? Keeping those decisions separate prevents silent emitters, doubled music, and sounds that survive a level change." relatedSlugs={["godot-trigger-system","animation-frame-events-godot","change-scenes-load-levels-godot"]} godotSource={{label:"AudioStreamPlayer3D class reference",href:"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_audiostreamplayer3d.html"}}>
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>Choose the right player:</strong> use <code>AudioStreamPlayer3D</code> for sound with a world position, <code>AudioStreamPlayer</code> for non-positional music and UI, and an audio bus for category-wide volume and effects.</div>
    <h2>Set up one positional sound</h2>
    <ol><li>Add an <code>AudioStreamPlayer3D</code> beneath the object that produces the sound.</li><li>Assign an imported audio stream and preview it before adding gameplay logic.</li><li>Set <strong>Max Distance</strong> and attenuation so the sound fades naturally instead of stopping at an arbitrary wall.</li><li>Route it to a named bus such as <code>SFX</code> or <code>Ambience</code>.</li><li>Enable autoplay only for persistent ambience. Start one-shot effects in response to a signal or gameplay event.</li></ol>
    <h2>Keep playback event-driven</h2>
    <p>A door, trigger, or animation should request a sound by meaning—<code>DOOR_OPENED</code> or <code>FOOTSTEP</code>—rather than searching for an audio node by a fragile scene path. The owning object can decide whether to restart, ignore, or vary the sound. Use animation frame events for exact timing and trigger enter/exit events for environmental loops.</p>
    <h2>Handle music separately</h2>
    <p>Background music usually belongs to a level or music manager, not a 3D object. Keep one authority responsible for crossfades and duplicate prevention. Decide whether music stops at a level boundary or persists across scenes, and test that decision during rapid transitions.</p>
    <h2>Debug silent or incorrect audio</h2>
    <ul><li>Confirm the stream path exists and the file imported successfully.</li><li>Check the player, its bus, and the bus mute/volume controls.</li><li>For 3D sound, confirm a current camera/listener exists and the source is inside its audible distance.</li><li>Check whether autoplay is actually intended or an event never fired.</li><li>When looping, verify the stream or player is configured to loop and that an exit event stops it.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>Mark a Blender object as a <strong>Speaker</strong>, set its Godot audio path, autoplay, loop, volume, and maximum distance, then export the GLB. FPE creates the positional player and cleans it up with the imported node. For level music, set <strong>Background Music</strong> in Scene Settings. Triggers, frame events, and Scene Event commands can start or stop speakers without object-specific scripts.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-audio.html">Read the complete FPE audio reference</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
